'''Marik Sahamin''' (born 5 June 1977) is a Burawi politician and electrical engineer who served as the [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] from 2015 to 2025. He is the founder and current leader of the [[Forward Party of Burawa]], and previously represented [[Badalga-6]] in the [[Lower House of Burawa]] from 2007 until his election to the Leadership. At age 38, he became the youngest Leader in Burawi history following his victory in the [[2015 Burawi leadership election]].

Sahamin's decade in office was marked by ambitious policy initiatives, including the recognition of [[same-sex marriage]] in 2016, the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]] which banned domestic commercial flights, the [[2022 Sabia operation]], and reforms to the immigration system. His foreign policy emphasised the concept of a "Burawi sphere of influence" — active economic and political management of neighbouring Kaftian states.

In February 2026, Sahamin was removed as party leader by a vote of no confidence. He announced his candidacy in the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|snap leadership election]] in March 2026, triggering a party split that resulted in the formation of the [[Forward Progressive Party]]. He is a candidate in the 2026 snap election.

== Early life and education ==

Marik Sahamin was born on 5 June 1977 in [[Badalga]], a major city in the [[Lake Haro]] region of Burawa. His mother, '''[[Amina Sahamin]]'', was a professor at the [[University of Badalga]]; his father was a civil servant. He was raised in the Badalga area and attended '''Badalga Central High School''', where he served as president of the student union.

After completing secondary education, Sahamin entered the [[Burawi Work Programme]] through the continued education track and enrolled at the [[People's University of Burawa]] in [[Kankadadka]], where he studied electrical engineering.

== Early career ==

Following graduation, Sahamin was hired by '''Qynos''', a multinational computer electronics company based in [[Khar]]. He worked in the technology sector during the early 2000s.

Sahamin became politically active in his early twenties, joining his local community council in Badalga. He subsequently served as a political aide to '''[[Siciid Barre]]''', the popular senator for Badalga, gaining experience in electoral politics and constituent services.

== Parliamentary career ==

=== Election to the Lower House ===

At age 28, Sahamin contested the [[Badalga-6]] legislative district in the [[2007 Burawi Lower House election]], winning his first term in the [[Lower House of Burawa]]. The district encompassed the neighbourhood where he had grown up. He was elected as a member of the [[Socialist Party of Burawa]] and aligned himself with the party's progressive wing.

=== Rise within the Socialist Party ===

Over the following years, Sahamin built support within the progressive faction of the Socialist Party. He advocated for faster reform on social issues, environmental policy, and government modernisation. By the early 2010s, tensions had emerged between the progressive wing and the party's establishment, which Sahamin and his allies criticised as overly cautious.

In 2014, Sahamin broke from the Socialist Party to found the [[Forward Party of Burawa]] on 4 July. The new party positioned itself as a vehicle for ambitious progressive action and attracted support from reformers, environmentalists, and younger voters.

== Leader of the Burawi Republic (2015–2025) ==

{{main|Leadership of Marik Sahamin}}

=== 2015 election ===

{{main|2015 Burawi leadership election}}

Sahamin contested the [[2015 Burawi leadership election]] as the Forward Party candidate. The election was a '''triple election''' in which the Leadership, the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]], and the [[Lower House of Burawa|Lower House]] were all contested simultaneously — a convergence that occurs every thirty years. At age 38, Sahamin won the election, becoming the youngest [[Leader of the Burawi Republic]] in Burawi history.

=== Domestic policy ===

One of the Forward Party's first legislative acts was the passage of a resolution recognising [[same-sex marriage]] in 2016. The Socialist Party, while supportive in principle, had declined to legislate marriage equality during its decades in power; Sahamin's government acted within its first year.

Sahamin's government passed the [[Clean Air and Transportation Act of 2020]], which banned most domestic commercial flights between Burawi cities in favour of the [[Burawi High Speed Rail|high-speed rail]] network. The legislation was criticised in rural areas, where residents were required to travel to metropolitan airports to access the rail network, and was later partially reversed by his successor, [[Hancil Thea]], through executive directive.

In 2019, Sahamin's government reformed the [[Immigration in Burawa|immigration system]], introducing a 90-day grace period for foreign workers who lost employment, replacing the previous requirement to leave the country and restart the ten-year citizenship path entirely.

=== Foreign policy ===

Sahamin pursued an activist foreign policy characterised by the concept of a '''Burawi sphere of influence''' — the active economic and political management of neighbouring Kaftian states and former colonies. The most controversial expression of this policy was the [[2022 Sabia operation]], a one-day military intervention to depose the president of [[Sabia]]. The operation generated significant domestic debate but succeeded in installing a government aligned with Burawi interests.

Sahamin also negotiated the [[Kwalib reparations]], addressing historical grievances with the neighbouring state.

=== Party consolidation ===

In 2020, the [[Progressive Socialist Party of Burawa]] — formerly the principal vehicle of the progressive coalition — formally merged with the Forward Party, consolidating much of the progressive electorate under Sahamin's leadership.

=== 2025 election defeat ===

{{main|2025 Burawi leadership election}}

Sahamin sought re-election in the [[2025 Burawi leadership election]], the first leadership election conducted under [[ranked-choice voting]]. He placed third in first-preference votes with 24 per cent, behind [[Xasan Garaad]] (30 per cent) and [[Hancil Thea]] (28 per cent). Under the RCV redistribution, Thea accumulated sufficient second-preference support to win the election. The result ended a decade of Forward Party government and marked the first time the [[National Party of Burawa]] had won the Leadership.

== Post-Leadership (2025–2026) ==

Following his electoral defeat, Sahamin remained the leader of the Forward Party and retained his seat representing Badalga-6. The Forward Party continued to hold a majority in the [[Senate of Burawa|Senate]] (128 seats), a legacy of its strength in the 2024 Senate election, though this was expected to diminish in the 2027 election.

=== Vote of no confidence ===

In February 2026, the Forward Party congress held a vote of no confidence in Sahamin's leadership, which passed. '''[[Warsame Cilmi]]''' was elected as his successor, pledging to refocus the party on its reformist identity. Sahamin remained a prominent figure within the party but no longer held the formal leadership.

In the weeks following his removal, Sahamin worked to regain political influence within the Forward Party. He negotiated an agreement to run as the party's candidate for Leader in a future election, but this arrangement was opposed by the faction that had supported his removal.

=== Snap election candidacy ===

{{main|2026 Burawi snap election}}

On 26 March 2026, following the successful recall of [[Hancil Thea]] from the Leadership, Sahamin announced his candidacy in the [[2026 Burawi snap leadership election|snap leadership election]]. The announcement triggered a major split within the Forward Party. Approximately 60 per cent of the party's parliamentary caucus — comprising primarily those who had supported the February no-confidence vote — defected to form the [[Forward Progressive Party]] under '''[[Mahad Allaale]]'''. 

Sahamin retained the support of approximately 40 per cent of the caucus and reclaimed the party leadership. The Forward Progressive Party announced it would not field its own candidate and instead endorsed [[Muna Xasan]] of the [[Party for Equitable Progress]]. The split left Sahamin's Forward Party with 51 Senate seats and 40 Lower House seats.

Sahamin has participated in televised debates on 8 April and 12 April 2026. Opinion polls have recorded his support at 10 per cent (7 April), 4 per cent (9 April), and 8 per cent (12 April), placing him fourth among the candidates.

== Political positions ==

Sahamin is associated with the progressive wing of Burawi politics. His positions include:

* '''Social progressivism''': Advocates for LGBTQ+ rights, feminist policy, and expanded social services.
* '''Environmentalism''': Championed the transition from domestic aviation to high-speed rail.
* '''Active foreign policy''': Supports Burawi intervention in neighbouring states to promote stability and socialist governance.
* '''Immigration reform''': Advocates for expanded worker protections within the immigration system.
* '''Economic modernisation''': Supports selective liberalisation in technology sectors while maintaining essential industries under state control.

== Personal life ==

Sahamin married '''[[Xalwo Geedi]]''', his college sweetheart, in 2000. The couple have two children and reside in [[Badalga]].

